    "designMd": "# Design System: Feedlot Operations Workspace\n\n## 1. Visual Theme & Atmosphere\nA calm, practical work surface for long hours of scanning, comparing, and checking operational records: **density 6/10, variance 4/10, motion 5/10**. Borrow the retail field’s white surfaces, plain chrome, dense scannable results, and single warm action color; translate its strong price hierarchy into clearly aligned cost and quantity figures. Depart from soft, spacious catalog layouts with compact tables, flat panels, and a restrained 8px corner system. Keep the look steady and legible rather than storefront-like: clear rules, concise headings, and strong status distinctions carry the hierarchy. All layouts share the same responsive browser interface, from phone widths to desktop.\n\n## 2. Color Palette & Roles\nUse only this palette throughout; neutral surfaces never take on the accent’s tint.\n- **Paper canvas** (`#F6F7F5`) — The single page background.\n- **White work surface** (`#FFFFFF`) — The single panel and field surface; also the light text on the primary action.\n- **Graphite ink** (`#252A27`) — The single primary text color.\n- **Quiet graphite** (`#66706A`) — The single muted text color for supporting details.\n- **Fine rule** (`#D9DEDA`) — The single divider and field-border color.\n- **Burnt action orange** (`#AD5C2A`) — The single accent, used only for primary actions and the selected menu item; never for a status.\n- **Field green** (`#28734F`) — Good status, always paired with explicit text.\n- **Harvest ochre** (`#94600C`) — Waiting status, always paired with explicit text.\n- **Brick red** (`#AE3D36`) — Refused or failed status, always paired with explicit text.\n\n## 3. Typography Rules\n- **Display and headings:** Hanken Grotesk, with controlled negative tracking (`-0.03em`) and weight doing more work than scale. H1 is 30px/650 on wide screens and 26px on phones; H2 is 22px/650; H3 is 17px/600.\n- **Body and controls:** Source Sans 3 at 16px with 1.5 line height; dense table text may reduce to 14px. Keep prose to 65 characters per line and never shrink phone body text below 16px.\n- **Numbers, codes, and times only:** IBM Plex Mono. Never use it for labels, statuses, or sentences. Apply `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums` to align figures in columns; format dates and currency consistently for the user’s locale.\n- **Type craft:** Sentence case for labels, headings, and buttons; uppercase only for table column heads, tracked by `0.08em`. Use curly quotation marks and en dashes for ranges. Do not use all-caps metadata as a decorative contrast.\n- **Banned:** Inter, generic serif faces, and serif pairings. This is a software interface; use sans-serif throughout.\n\n## 4. Component Stylings\n- **Buttons:** Primary actions use a solid burnt-orange fill and white text; secondary actions use the white surface, graphite text, and a 1px rule. Both are at least 44px tall with a 6px corner radius. Hover changes opacity, keyboard focus gets a visible 2px graphite outline with a 2px offset, and press scales to `0.97` for 90ms. Keep the accent exclusive to actions and selection.\n- **Panels and cards:** Prefer flat, unshadowed work surfaces edged with a 1px fine rule. Use a panel only when it separates a meaningful work area; do not turn each fact into a card. Outer panels use an 8px radius; nested fields and controls use 6px.\n- **Tables and filters:** Keep dense tables calm and sortable at a glance, with right-aligned quantities and costs, a consistent 48px desktop row, and a 1px horizontal rule between rows. Put field labels above controls; controls are at least 44px tall. On phones, reflow records into stacked, labeled rows rather than creating horizontal overflow.\n- **Status:** Show good, waiting, and refused/failed states with their dedicated color, explicit text, and a fine matching outline. Color never carries status meaning alone; do not use the action accent for status.\n- **Errors and loading:** Place clear error text directly below the affected field. Use layout-matched skeletons only while content is loading; no generic circular spinners.\n- **Empty states:** Give a concise, plain-language explanation and one useful next action. Do not use decorative illustrations or filler copy.\n- **Photography:** Any depicted person, place, product, vehicle, or animal is a real photograph, never a decorative stock scene. Use a consistent 4:3 crop with the subject centered; reuse the same photograph wherever the same person recurs. Images identify something, rather than decorate a surface.\n- **Icons:** Use one restrained SVG icon set only where an icon speeds recognition; keep a consistent 1.5px stroke and 16px/20px sizes. Text remains the carrier of meaning; no emojis or icon on every row.\n\n## 5. Layout Principles\n- Use CSS Grid to keep titles, controls, and data aligned to a shared set of columns; constrain the main work area to 1,360px and let tables use available width. The title occupies a deliberately narrow left region while the data area stays broad.\n- Keep spacing on a 4px base rhythm: 8px, 12px, 16px, 24px, and 32px are the main steps. Use hairline rules and whitespace instead of shadows for separation.\n- Avoid equal three-card feature rows and repeated card grids. Let content determine the rhythm: broad tables, narrow explanatory blocks, and open whitespace have distinct roles.\n- **Responsive:** Below 768px, multi-column layouts become one column. Phone tables become stacked record rows, controls remain at least 44px, and no page may scroll horizontally. Tablet layouts retain the broad data-first structure while simplifying secondary columns. Desktop chrome may become a compact browser menu on phones.\n- Scale headings with `clamp()`, maintain body text at 16px or larger on phones, and reduce vertical section gaps proportionally. This is a responsive web app, not a native-app frame.\n\n## 6. Motion & Interaction\nAll timing, CSS easing, entrance choreography, interaction states, and the single live indicator are defined in §9; do not add motion outside that budget. Movement should explain a data change or direct input, never act as decoration. The reduced-motion behavior in §9 is mandatory.\n\n## 7. Anti-Patterns (Banned)\n- No emojis, custom cursors, neon accents, outer glows, gradient text, gradient meshes, blobs, glass effects, or decorative shadows.\n- No pure black, extra palette colors, accent-colored statuses, or status conveyed by color alone.\n- No Inter, generic serif faces, placeholder copy, invented metrics, fake round-number claims, or AI copywriting clichés.\n- No equal three-column card grids, repeated icon tiles, logo strips, decorative illustrations, or icons attached to every item.\n- No overlapping text and images, text parallax, scroll-jacking, far-off fly-ins, bouncing controls, or pulsing actions.\n- No horizontal overflow on phones and no content that remains hidden until an entrance animation finishes.\n\n## 8. Voice & Font Rationale\n- **Voice:** practical, exact, steady.\n- **Display:** Hanken Grotesk — its sturdy, distinct letterforms make headings feel assured without shouting (Google Fonts fallback: Hanken Grotesk).\n- **Body / Mono:** Source Sans 3 keeps dense reading open and familiar; IBM Plex Mono gives figures, codes, and times clean alignment without turning prose into a console.\n\n## 9. Motion System\n- **Motion level:** restrained and purposeful; CSS-only, with no motion that depends on a script to explain state.\n- **Tokens:** `--dur-instant: 90ms`; `--dur-quick: 160ms`; `--dur-base: 260ms`; `--dur-slow: 420ms`; `--dur-scene: 800ms`; `--ease-out: cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1)`; `--ease-in: cubic-bezier(0.7, 0, 0.84, 0)`; `--ease-inout: cubic-bezier(0.65, 0, 0.35, 1)`; `--ease-spring: linear(0, 0.009, 0.035 2.1%, 0.141, 0.281 6.7%, 0.723 12.9%, 0.938 16.7%, 1.017, 1.077 21.4%, 1.121 24%, 1.149 27%, 1.159, 1.154 33%, 1.051 43.2%, 1.013 49.5%, 0.996 57%, 1.001 72%, 1)`; `--travel: clamp(8px, 1vw, 16px)`.\n- **Entrance:** On initial page load, reveal the title area, controls, then the first visible data group with a small opacity change and at most 8px of upward travel. Stagger items by 60ms; after six items, reveal the remainder as a group. Complete the scene within 800ms; content stays visible throughout.\n- **Scroll-linked:** Only a real chart or progress visualization may use `animation-timeline: view()` and `animation-range: entry 0% entry 40%`; its restrained horizontal scale-in conveys measured progress entering the reading area. Do not fade every section on scroll, and reveal only once.\n- **Interaction feedback:** Buttons use opacity on hover, a visible graphite focus outline, and a `scale(0.97)` press. Table rows tint only with the existing surface/rule treatment; recognized inline icons nudge at most 3px. Use the 90ms, 160ms, and 260ms tokens for press, hover/focus, and state changes respectively.\n- **State transitions:** Moving indicators use `transform` with the in-out easing. Expandable regions use `grid-template-rows: 0fr` to `1fr`; inserted or reordered rows use a 160ms fade and 8px shift. Use a shared-element View Transition for a matching record title or image where supported.\n- **Ambient (max 1):** A live performance dot alone breathes gently between 0.96 and 1 opacity on an 8s cycle; screens without a live indicator have no ambient loop.\n- **Signature moment — Filtered row settle:** Updated table rows resolve with a short, grouped arrival rather than a page-wide reveal: 160ms opacity and an 8px transform, staggered by 50ms for up to six rows.\n- **Reduced motion:** Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, remove entrances, scroll-linked motion, and ambient loops; content remains visible, while state changes keep no more than a 120ms fade.\n\n```css\n:root {\n  --dur-instant: 90ms;\n  --dur-quick: 160ms;\n  --dur-base: 260ms;\n  --dur-slow: 420ms;\n  --dur-scene: 800ms;\n  --ease-out: cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1);\n  --ease-in: cubic-bezier(0.7, 0, 0.84, 0);\n  --ease-inout: cubic-bezier(0.65, 0, 0.35, 1);\n  --ease-spring: linear(0, 0.009, 0.035 2.1%, 0.141, 0.281 6.7%, 0.723 12.9%, 0.938 16.7%, 1.017, 1.077 21.4%, 1.121 24%, 1.149 27%, 1.159, 1.154 33%, 1.051 43.2%, 1.013 49.5%, 0.996 57%, 1.001 72%, 1);\n  --travel: clamp(8px, 1vw, 16px);\n}\n@keyframes row-arrive {\n  from { opacity: 0.82; transform: translateY(8px); }\n  to { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }\n}\n@keyframes status-breathe {\n  0%, 100% { opacity: 0.96; }\n  50% { opacity: 1; }\n}\n.result-row { animation: row-arrive var(--dur-quick) var(--ease-out) both; }\n.result-row:nth-child(2) { animation-delay: 50ms; }\n.result-row:nth-child(3) { animation-delay: 100ms; }\n.result-row:nth-child(4) { animation-delay: 150ms; }\n.result-row:nth-child(5) { animation-delay: 200ms; }\n.result-row:nth-child(6) { animation-delay: 250ms; }\n.live-indicator { animation: status-breathe 8s ease-in-out infinite; }\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n  *, *::before, *::after {\n    animation: none !important;\n    scroll-behavior: auto !important;\n    transition-duration: 120ms !important;\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## 10. Component Substitutions\n- Replace icon-in-rounded-square feature cards and equal tile grids with calm, labeled rows, compact tables, and clear rules.\n- Replace dashboard KPI-card clusters with one clearly emphasized measure where needed, followed by the dense data that gives it context.\n- Replace logo strips, decorative illustrations, and generic testimonial or pricing layouts with nothing; they do not belong in this operational workspace.\n- Use real, consistently cropped photography only when it identifies an item, animal, person, place, or vehicle; never use imagery as a decorative backdrop.\n\n## 11. Human Fingerprint\n- **Unexpected decision:** Keep the page title in a deliberately narrow left column while the data region opens wide on the same grid. This ledger-like asymmetry makes dense work feel ordered without borrowing a storefront layout.\n- **Radius family / elevation levels / grid:** Soft 8px outer corners and 6px nested controls; panels use a 1px rule and no shadow; one shared CSS Grid alignment keeps edges deliberate.\n- **Image treatment / icon set:** Real identifying photography, consistent 4:3 centered crops, and the same photograph for a recurring person; a single restrained SVG set at 1.5px stroke, 16px/20px sizes.\n",
